					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/metaphysician" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1698079); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1698079&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606142682" alt="Metaphysician" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1698079)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/metaphysician" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1698079); return false;" >Metaphysician</a></span> <span class="odate time_1452373725 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Jan 2016 21:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2436999">
									

<p>It isn't so much sad that it can't die. It doesn't understand quite why it remains and others keep going away. It knows that it is special but doesn't fully grasp the ramifications of that. Time, I imagine, is also perceived quite differently by it. It understands &quot;before&quot; and &quot;now&quot; but has no real time frame in which to anchor itself. It doesn't understand that it is immortal. All it knows is others go away but never itself. It is quite different from how we would view immortality. It doesn't understand it in a larger scale. It is, however, able to understand that what it reanimates aren't the same. That even when moving, there is a &quot;change&quot;; I'm trying to explore how it would come to view these things, within the limits of its intellect.</p>

							</div>

								
